  "description_html": "<p>Restoration of <i>Falcarius utahensins</i>, based on images of skeletons.<sup id=\"cite_ref-1\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-1\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">[</span>1<span class=\"cite-bracket\">]</span></a></sup> <i>Falcarius</i> is a member of therizinosauria (Dinosauria, Theropoda, Coelurosauria) from the Early Cretaceous of <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Utah\" title=\"Utah\">Utah</a> (<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/USA\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"USA\">USA</a>). It have often been hailed as a \"missing link\" between the herbivorous therizinosaurs of the Late Cretaceous and their carnivorous ancestors, as it possess more features in common with theropod dinosaurs in general than Late Createous therizinosaurids do.\n</p>\n<div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading2\"><h2 id=\"References\">References</h2></div>\n<ol class=\"references\">\n<li id=\"cite_note-1\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-1\">↑</a></span> <span class=\"reference-text\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"http://img471.imageshack.us/img471/2671/falcaaj0.jpg\">[1]</a>, <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"http://web.utah.edu/news/news_images_2005/may/kirkland1.jpg\">[2]</a></span>\n</li>\n</ol>",
